            <fr:title text="generalized element">generalized element</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
    A <html:em>generalized element</html:em> (alt. local element) of an object <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A ∈
    \mathcal {C}]]></fr:tex> is <html:em>just</html:em> a morphism <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[e : B → A]]></fr:tex>, for some <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>. Ie, a
    generalized element of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is <html:em>just</html:em> an object of the slice over <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    There's supposed to be some Kripke-style interpretation of these as
    elements defined at points in time, but TODO.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    From <fr:link href="/mclarty-1992/" title="Elementary Categories, Elementary Toposes" uri="https://hejohns.github.io/mclarty-1992/" display-uri="mclarty-1992" type="local">Elementary Categories, Elementary Toposes</fr:link>. Compare with <fr:link href="/hejohns-000K/" title="global element" uri="https://hejohns.github.io/hejohns-000K/" display-uri="hejohns-000K" type="local">global element</fr:link>.
</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
